# Line endings: store LF in the repository, check out LF on every platform.
#
# This machine has core.autocrlf=true and no .gitattributes, which made
# every fresh checkout rewrite source files to CRLF. That contradicts
# .editorconfig (end_of_line = lf) and produced a 53-file warning on the
# first commit. It also means the first person on macOS or Linux gets a
# different working tree than the first person on Windows.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect; `eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree
# regardless of the user's core.autocrlf setting.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that must never be line-ending normalized.
#
# The wrapper JAR is the important one: Git rewriting a byte inside
# gradle-wrapper.jar produces a corrupt JAR and a "Could not validate
# distribution" error that looks like a bad download rather than a
# checkout problem.
*.jar binary
*.jks binary
*.keystore binary
*.apk binary
*.aab binary
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.so binary
*.9.png binary
gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar binary

# Markdown: keep it LF too, but be explicit because the docs are
# line-ending sensitive (cross-references span lines).
*.md text eol=lf

# Gradle/Kotlin/Java/SQL are all covered by `text=auto eol=lf` above.
# Listing them is redundant but makes the intent greppable when someone
# adds a new module and wonders whether the default is safe.
*.kts text eol=lf
*.kt text eol=lf
*.java text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.pro text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
